.Dd $Mdocdate: November 16 2026 $
.Dt IMAPIDLE.CONF 5
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm imapidle.conf
.Nd imapidle configuration file
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Pa ~/.config/imapidle/imapidle.conf
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
tells
.Sy imapidle(1)
which IMAP accounts to watch and which
.Sy mbsync
channel to run when mail changes.
The file is INI.
Every section has a
.Va type
of
.Cm global ,
.Cm server ,
.Cm account ,
.Cm idle ,
or
.Cm pulse .
The comparison ignores case.
The section name is a label used in log lines.
At most one
.Cm global
section is allowed.
A missing
.Va type ,
an unknown
.Va type ,
or a second
.Cm global
section rejects the file.
.Cm server
names a host, a port, and how the session is encrypted.
.Cm account
holds a login and an
.Sy mbsync
channel and does not connect until an
.Cm idle
or
.Cm pulse
section names it.
.Cm idle
watches mailboxes of one account.
.Cm pulse
lists accounts whose mailboxes are swept on a timer.
More than one
.Cm pulse
section is allowed, and each has its own sweep.
.Cm global
holds the limits and the tunnel switch.
See
.Sx SERVERS ,
.Sx PULSE CHANNELS ,
and
.Sx TRACKED MAILBOXES .
.Pp
The directory should be mode 0700 and the file mode 0600.
A
.Va pass
value is stored in the file.
A
.Va pass_cmd
value is not a place to hide a secret: anyone who can read the file can
read the command.
.Pp
Lines that start with
.Ql #
or
.Ql ;
are comments.
Text after a value is not a comment.
.Pp
An
.Cm account
section, and an
.Cm idle
section that names
.Va server
itself, requires
.Va server ,
.Va user ,
and exactly one of
.Va pass
or
.Va pass_cmd .
That inline
.Cm idle
section also requires
.Va mbsync_channel .
An
.Cm account
section uses its own name when
.Va mbsync_channel
is omitted.
An
.Cm idle
section may instead set
.Va channel
to an account name and put
.Va folderN
there.
An
.Cm idle
section that sets neither
.Va channel
nor a login is missing
.Va channel .
A
.Cm pulse
.Va channelN
value is an account name.
A missing required key, both
.Va pass
and
.Va pass_cmd ,
a bad port, a bad
.Va proto ,
an empty
.Va folderN ,
a resync time below 60 seconds,
.Va folderN
on an
.Cm account
or
.Cm pulse
section,
or a bad
.Cm pulse
or
.Cm global
section
is written to standard error.
.Nm imapidle
then exits 1 and does not become a daemon.
A section with
.Va disabled
set to
.Cm yes
is kept and is not connected.
Required keys are not checked for that section.
Any other value leaves the section active.
The old names
.Va password
and
.Va password_cmd
are not accepted.
.Sh KEYS
.Bl -tag -width "mbsync_channel"
.It Va type
.Cm global ,
.Cm server ,
.Cm account ,
.Cm idle ,
or
.Cm pulse .
Required.
The comparison ignores case.
.It Va disabled
Set to
.Cm yes
to keep the section without connecting to it.
The comparison ignores case.
.It Va server
On an
.Cm account
or an inline
.Cm idle
section, either the name of a
.Cm server
section or a host name.
A host name uses implicit TLS on port 993 unless this section
sets
.Va port
or
.Va proto .
Required for those logins.
.It Va host
Host name of a
.Cm server
section.
Required there.
.It Va port
TCP port.
When it is omitted,
.Cm tls
uses 993 and
.Cm starttls
or
.Cm notls
uses 143.
A port written on the
.Cm server
section stays when an account changes
.Va proto ,
unless that account also sets
.Va port .
.It Va proto
.Cm tls ,
.Cm starttls ,
or
.Cm notls .
.Cm tls
is implicit TLS immediately after TCP and is the default.
.Cm starttls
reads the clear greeting, requests STARTTLS, and then uses
the same certificate checks.
.Cm notls
stays cleartext, for a local host or a forwarded port, and
records no certificate.
The default is never
.Cm notls .
An account may set its own value.
Any other word rejects the file.
.It Va user
IMAP login name.
Required.
.It Va pass
The IMAP password, used as-is.
Set this or
.Va pass_cmd ,
not both.
The value is not written to the log.
Required unless
.Va pass_cmd
is set.
.It Va pass_cmd
A command passed to
.Pa /bin/sh
with
.Fl c .
The first line of its standard output, without the trailing newline, is
the IMAP password.
The command must exit 0.
Empty output, a non-zero exit, or no exit within 30 seconds fails that
account only.
Required unless
.Va pass
is set.
The command is run again on each connection, so a helper that prints a
fresh access token can be dropped in here.
The command text and its output are not written to the log.
Standard error from the command is discarded.
.It Va auth
How
.Va pass ,
or the first line from
.Va pass_cmd ,
is presented to the server.
.Cm login
is the default and sends IMAP
.Cm LOGIN .
.Cm xoauth2
sends
.Cm AUTHENTICATE XOAUTH2 .
For
.Cm xoauth2
the value is a bearer token.
The token is not written to the log.
.It Va channel
On an
.Cm idle
section, the account to watch.
The idle section name is only a label.
.Va folderN
belongs on this section.
Setting both
.Va channel
and
.Va server
rejects the file.
On a
.Cm pulse
section the numbered form
.Va channelN
names accounts.
A bare
.Va channel
key there rejects the file.
.It Va mbsync_channel
Channel name passed to
.Sy mbsync .
An inline
.Cm idle
section requires it.
An
.Cm account
section defaults it to the section name.
It must match a channel in the
.Sy mbsync
configuration.
Two idle or pulse accounts may not use the same name.
While
.Va tunnel
is off, that file is not read.
While
.Va tunnel
is on, it is read so matching accounts can be pointed at the
helper.
.It Va wait
Seconds to sleep inside the
.Sy mbsync
child before exec.
The default is 0.
This does not stall the IMAP loop.
.It Va hook
A command passed to
.Pa /bin/sh
with
.Fl c ,
run after an
.Sy mbsync
of this section's channel exits 0.
One exit runs it once.
The same command already running runs once more when it exits.
The command text is not written to the log.
Standard input, output, and error are discarded.
A non-zero exit from the hook is logged and does not disable
the account.
Unset means no command.
.It Va poll
Seconds between mailbox checks when the server advertises
neither
.Cm IDLE
nor
.Cm NOTIFY .
The default is 60.
A value below 60 is rejected.
The account keeps one connection and sends
.Cm STATUS
for each configured folder.
A server that supports either extension ignores this key.
.It Va hyperdelete
.Cm yes
starts a sync when a watched folder reports a removal.
Any other value, or omitting the key, leaves that removal until
the next arrival or the folder's resync time.
The comparison ignores case.
The global section sets the default for an idle account that
omits the key.
An idle section that names the key uses its own value.
A reload applies it without reconnecting.
.It Va folder0 , folder1 , ...
Mailbox names, starting at
.Va folder0
with no gaps.
A value is a mailbox name, and may add a priority and a resync
time, separated by semicolons.
.Bd -literal -offset indent
name
name;priority=N
name;priority=N;seconds
name;priority;seconds
.Ed
.Pp
A missing priority is 5.
The two-field form is
.Ar name
or
.Ar name;priority=N .
A two-field suffix other than
.Ar priority=N
leaves the priority at 5, so
.Ar INBOX;1
stays at 5.
A bare priority number is the middle field when a resync time
follows it, as in
.Ar INBOX.2026-11;5;3600 .
An unrecognized priority word is ignored and the priority stays 5.
The resync time is whole seconds, at least 60, and defaults to
43200, which is 12 hours.
A smaller value rejects the file.
The priority gap still applies, so a short resync time waits out
that gap before the folder is synced.
.It Va folder
Used only when no
.Va folderN
key is present, and only for
.Cm idle .
The default mailbox is
.Cm INBOX .
.Va folder
or
.Va folderN
on an
.Cm account
or
.Cm pulse
section rejects the file.
.It Va priority
Used only with
.Va folder ,
not with
.Va folderN .
The default is 5.
.El
.Pp
Every folder in a section shares that section's
.Va mbsync_channel ,
.Va user ,
and
.Va pass .
.Sh PRIORITY
Priority is the minimum time between
.Sy mbsync
runs for that folder:
.Bl -column "priority" "seconds" -offset indent
.It Sy priority Ta Sy seconds
.It 1 or less Ta 7
.It 2 Ta 42
.It 3 Ta 128
.It 4 Ta 256
.It 5 Ta 420
.It 6 Ta 540
.It 7 Ta 660
.It 8 Ta 780
.It 9 or more Ta 3600
.El
.Pp
A folder is ready when it has never been included in a run, or when the
minimum time since its last run has passed.
An arrival for a ready folder starts a 5 second wait on that
.Va mbsync_channel .
Arrivals for other folders of the same channel during those 5 seconds
join one invocation, and do not start the 5 seconds over:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
mbsync channel:INBOX channel:Archive
.Ed
.Pp
An arrival before the minimum time has passed is kept.
When the minimum time expires,
.Nm imapidle
schedules
.Sy mbsync
for that folder on the next wake.
That scheduling does not add another 5 seconds, unless the channel
already has a combine window open, in which case the folder joins it.
.Cm INBOX
at priority 5 is included in a run.
A further arrival for that mailbox during the next 420 seconds does not
start another run immediately.
At the end of those 420 seconds the folder is scheduled.
If no new mail arrives during those 420 seconds, the folder stays
quiet until new mail arrives or its resync time elapses.
Either one starts the 5 second wait when the priority gap has
passed.
.Pp
The
.Va wait
value is an extra delay inside the
.Sy mbsync
child, after the 5 second wait.
A batch uses the largest
.Va wait
among its folders.
.Sh SERVERS
A section with
.Va type
.Cm server
is not connected by itself.
Other sections name it with
.Va server .
It needs
.Va host .
.Va proto
is
.Cm tls ,
.Cm starttls ,
or
.Cm notls ,
and
.Cm tls
is the default.
An omitted
.Va port
is 993 for
.Cm tls
and 143 for the other two.
A port that is set is used as written.
An account that names the section may set its own
.Va port
or
.Va proto .
Changing
.Va proto
picks the default port only when neither the account nor the
server wrote a port.
A
.Va server
value that is not a section name is a host name, with implicit
TLS on port 993 unless that account sets
.Va port
or
.Va proto .
.Cm notls
logs one line that the account is cleartext and skips the
certificate check.
.Sh PULSE CHANNELS
A section with
.Va type
.Cm pulse
names account sections, not bare
.Sy mbsync
channels.
The section may use any name.
More than one such section is allowed.
Each has its own
.Va sweep .
.Va channel0 ,
.Va channel1 ,
and so on start at 0 and have no gaps.
Each value is the name of an
.Cm account
section.
A name that is not an account rejects the file.
The same account on two pulse sections, or on both an idle
section and a pulse section, rejects the file.
Two of those accounts may not share one
.Va mbsync_channel ,
because the track file keys a mailbox by that channel.
A value may not contain a space or a colon, and may not start
with a dash.
The same name twice in one section is an error.
.Va folderN
rejects the file.
.Pp
The account connects for
.Cm LIST
and
.Cm STATUS
only.
It does not enter
.Cm IDLE .
Every mailbox that list returns is a pulse mailbox and runs as
.Pa mbsync channel:box .
One mailbox of a channel runs at a time.
Different channels run together, up to the free slots.
.Pp
.Va sweep
is how many seconds a mailbox that has mail waits after its own
.Sy mbsync
exits.
The default is 3600.
The next wake is that exit plus
.Va sweep ,
with no extra spread.
The first time a mailbox is seen with mail, and no earlier exit
is saved for that channel, the wait is
.Va sweep
plus the same quarter spread used by backoff, so a herd of new
mailboxes does not sync in the same second.
An exit recorded in
.Pa ~/.local/imapidle/pulse-last
seeds only a mailbox that is not already in the track file.
If that saved time plus
.Va sweep
is already past, the mailbox syncs now.
If it is still ahead, that time is the clock, and no second
spread is added.
A mailbox that is already tracked keeps the clock in the track
file.
.Pp
An empty pulse mailbox uses the tracked empty interval and the
weekly confirmation, not
.Va sweep .
See
.Sx TRACKED MAILBOXES .
A mailbox that gains mail leaves that class, and
.Va sweep
applies from the exit of the sync that finds the mail.
.Cm STATUS
runs before each sweep.
A due
.Cm STATUS
does not take an
.Sy mbsync
slot.
The weekly confirmation does, because it runs
.Sy mbsync .
.Pp
Named
.Va folderN
mailboxes are still queued at startup.
When
.Va connections
is above 1 and a pulse mailbox is due to sync, one slot stays
with those folders and pulse uses the other slots.
With one slot, that sync waits until the named sync is done.
When no pulse sync is due, the named folders may use every slot.
A mailbox that is still running is not started again.
.Pp
.Va hook
on this section is a command passed to
.Pa /bin/sh
with
.Fl c .
It runs when one of these mailboxes exits 0, unless that
account has its own
.Va hook .
The command text is not logged.
.Pp
These runs do not pause IMAP, the 5 second combine window, or a
different channel.
.Pp
.Va disabled
set to
.Cm yes
holds the section.
.Va sweep
and
.Va channelN
are not checked while it is held.
A file that holds only that section still loads.
Any other value leaves it active.
.Pp
On reload, a mailbox that remains keeps the clock it already has.
A new
.Va sweep
is stored on that mailbox and applies the next time it is
scheduled.
A channel that leaves pulse drops the mailboxes this sweep added.
.Sh TRACKED MAILBOXES
A section with
.Va type
.Cm global
is not an account.
The section may use any name.
With no global section, the defaults below are in force and tracking
is on.
A present section overrides only the keys it sets.
.Va disabled
set to
.Cm yes
stops tracked syncs.
.Va connections
still limits hot folders, tracked mailboxes, and pulse channels.
Any other value for
.Va disabled
leaves tracking active.
.Va hyperdelete
set to
.Cm yes
is the default for an idle account that omits the key.
.Pp
Values are whole numbers.
.Va min ,
.Va list ,
.Va id ,
.Va capability ,
.Va empty ,
and
.Va confirm
must be at least 60.
.Va max
must be at least
.Va min .
.Va capability
must be at least
.Va id .
.Va messages
and
.Va connections
must be at least 1.
A bad value rejects the file.
.Bl -tag -width "tunnel_timeout"
.It Va min
Minimum seconds between tracked syncs.
The default is 600.
.It Va messages
How many new messages one check should gather, until
.Va min
is reached.
The default is 5.
New messages are the Far and Near counts
.Sy mbsync
prints on the summary line.
.It Va initial
Seconds after the first sync of a mailbox, and the start of the
interval.
The default is 1800.
.It Va max
Longest tracked interval, in seconds.
The default is 21600.
.It Va empty
Seconds before a mailbox with no messages is asked again.
The default is 86400, one day.
.It Va confirm
Seconds before an empty mailbox is synced once, to catch
mail added on this machine.
The default is 604800, seven days.
The first assignment, and each later confirmation, adds
the same quarter spread used when a tracked wait grows.
.It Va list
Seconds between mailbox lists on the primary IMAP connection.
The default is 259200, three days.
A folder the server drops is logged and not synced until a later list
includes it.
.It Va id
Seconds between
.Cm ID
commands when the server software is not changing.
The default is 604800, seven days.
A changed reply hashes differently, requests
.Cm CAPABILITY ,
and halves this interval down toward
.Va min .
Three unchanged checks double it, up to
.Va capability .
.It Va capability
Seconds between
.Cm CAPABILITY
commands when the ID hash is unchanged.
The default is 604800.
.It Va connections
How many
.Sy mbsync
processes may run at once.
The default is 2.
This does not count IMAP connections.
When this is above 1 and a pulse channel is due, one slot stays
with the named folders and pulse uses the others.
With one slot, pulse waits until that named sync is done.
When no pulse channel is due, hot folders may use every slot.
Tracked mailboxes and pulse channels leave one free when this is
above 1 and no named sync is using it.
.It Va tunnel
.Cm yes
or
.Cm no .
The default is
.Cm no .
.Cm yes
runs
.Sy mbsync
through a helper that has already logged in.
See
.Sy imapidle(1) .
This is independent of
.Va disabled .
.It Va tunnel_timeout
Whole seconds for the helper login and for the generated
.Sy mbsync
.Cm Timeout .
The default is 3600.
A value below 1 is rejected.
.It Va mbsyncrc
Path of the
.Sy mbsync
configuration to copy when
.Va tunnel
is on.
Empty uses
.Pa $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/isyncrc
when that file exists, otherwise
.Pa ~/.mbsyncrc .
.Ev XDG_CONFIG_HOME
defaults to
.Pa ~/.config .
.It Va hook
A command passed to
.Pa /bin/sh
with
.Fl c ,
run after every
.Sy mbsync
that exits 0.
An account or pulse
.Va hook
for that run also runs when its text differs.
The same command already running runs once more when it exits.
Shutdown does not wait for it, and it does not use a connection
slot.
The command text is not written to the log.
.Va disabled
does not suppress this command.
An example is
.Cm notmuch new .
.El
.Pp
Every three finished syncs of one mailbox replace its interval.
Let M be the new messages, S the sum of the three intervals, and D
the average sync seconds.
No messages doubles the interval.
Otherwise the next wait is the larger of
.Va messages
times S/M and three times D.
When that result is longer than the current interval, one
quarter of the added seconds is X.
A random value modulo X is added so mailboxes that share the
math do not share a wake.
The result is then clamped to
.Va min
and
.Va max .
Three finishes with no summary double the interval the same way.
The learned interval is kept in memory and starts again from
.Va initial
after a restart.
The mailbox names are what the folder file keeps.
.Pp
When a tracked mailbox is due, the primary connection sends
.Cm STATUS
for
.Cm MESSAGES
and
.Cm UIDNEXT
before
.Sy mbsync
runs.
Zero messages and the same
.Cm UIDNEXT
wait
.Va empty
seconds and do not sync.
The first answer of zero messages does that too.
A larger count, or a new
.Cm UIDNEXT ,
syncs and then uses the three-sample interval.
The saved
.Cm UIDNEXT
lasts for this process.
.Pp
.Pa ~/tmp/pause ,
.Pa ~/tmp/pause.mail ,
and
.Pa ~/tmp/pause.imapidle
hold new
.Sy mbsync
runs while any one of them exists.
.Pa ~/tmp/shutdown
and the same two suffixes shut the daemon down after the current
.Sy mbsync
processes exit.
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa ~/.config/imapidle/imapidle.conf
Default path.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/
State directory, mode 0700.
The configuration file stays in
.Pa ~/.config/imapidle/ .
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/known_hosts
SHA256 certificate fingerprints.
A current line is
.Pa server:port
and the fingerprint.
A retired line adds the GMT time it was replaced, as
.Ql YYYYMMDDTHH:MM:SSZ .
One line per host has no time.
If this file is missing and one exists beside the configuration, that
file is copied once and the old path is left in place.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/certs/
One directory per local year.
Each file is one certificate chain from a TLS handshake.
The name is the SHA1 of the file, a hyphen, and
.Pa host:port ,
for example
.Pa 2026/f0cbd2da7e9abbcce6171027bd69f28165468420-mail0.fdh.bz:993 .
The text matches
.Cm openssl s_client -showcerts .
The same chain is not stored twice.
The file mode is 0600.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/tls-negotiations/
One directory per local year.
Each file is what this client offered and what the server
selected.
The name is the SHA1 of the file, a hyphen, and
.Pa host:port .
Ephemeral keys, tickets, and session data are left out, so
the same server keeps the same file until the client or the
server changes the handshake.
The file mode is 0600.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/learned
What each server did with
.Cm NOTIFY ,
plus the hashed
.Cm ID
reply and when to ask again.
A line is
.Pa server:port ,
then
.Cm absent ,
.Cm rejected ,
or
.Cm active .
A rejected or active server may add the name it reported.
Delete a line to probe that server again.
An older file beside the configuration is copied once.
The file mode is 0600 when
.Nm imapidle
creates it.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/folders/
One file per channel, one mailbox name per line.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/track
The next wake, the interval, and
.Cm UIDNEXT
of each tracked mailbox, including a pulse mailbox, and when
the next folder list is due.
Written on shutdown.
A later start uses those clocks.
The file mode is 0600 when
.Nm imapidle
creates it.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/pulse-last
The last time an
.Sy mbsync
of a pulse channel exited.
One channel and that time per line.
A mailbox already stored in
.Pa track
keeps its own clock.
A mailbox seen for the first time uses this time as the start
of its sweep.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/mbsync-real
Count and total real seconds for each
.Sy mbsync
command.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/conn-time
Count and total connected seconds for each IMAP session.
.It Pa ~/.local/imapidle/conn-limit
How many IMAP sessions this user may open on a server and port.
One line is the server, the port, the user, that count, the next
time one extra session may be tried, and the server's own number
when the refusal included one.
A reload leaves the table in place.
.Sy imapidlectl(1)
can set the count and the time of the next try.
The file mode is 0600 when
.Nm imapidle
creates it.
.El
.Sh EXAMPLES
Two servers.
An omitted
.Va port
on
.Cm tls
is 993:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[mail]
type = server
host = mail.example.com
proto = tls

[office]
type = server
host = outlook.office365.com
proto = tls
.Ed
.Pp
Three accounts name those servers.
An omitted
.Va mbsync_channel
is the section name.
.Sy pizauth show work
is only an example of a command that prints the password:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[personal]
type = account
server = mail
user = you@example.com
pass = secret

[work]
type = account
server = mail
user = you@work.example
pass_cmd = pizauth show work

[lists]
type = account
server = office
user = you@lists.example
pass = secret
.Ed
.Pp
Watching starts when an idle or pulse section names the account.
Two idle sections each set
.Va channel
to one account and list that account's folders.
The section name is only a label.
.Va folder2
is priority 5 and resyncs after 3600 seconds:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[hot]
type = idle
channel = personal
folder0 = INBOX;priority=1
folder1 = Archive;priority=9
folder2 = INBOX.2026-11;5;3600

[desk]
type = idle
channel = work
folder0 = INBOX;priority=3
.Ed
.Pp
A pulse section names the remaining account.
The mailbox list comes from the server:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
[slow]
type = pulse
sweep = 7200
